      title: "Copy Element #4, Paragraph Headings: they make the copy look easy, and that's nearly all they do",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 18 · Powerful Copy Elements Explained · #4 Paragraph Headings · pp. 114–115 (PDF 139–140)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "The primary purpose of paragraph headings is to get the reader to read the copy by making the copy look less intimidating.", page: "115" }
      ],
      idea: "Paragraph headings appear twice in the book: as graphic element #6 in Chapter 4, and here as a copy element. Their job is the same in both places. They make the copy look less intimidating, so the reader starts reading.\n\n**Why the look matters.** A long ad that looks like one unbroken block feels hard before anyone reads a word. The same copy broken into neat chunks, each with a heading, feels easy. That's a gut reaction, and it decides whether reading starts at all.\n\n**What a heading can say.** It can introduce the paragraph below it. Or, Sugarman says, it can have nothing to do with the paragraph or even the whole ad. His proof is a test. In an ad for a radar speed indicator, he used absurd headings such as \"Scrambled Eggs\" and \"Success and Good Things.\" Nobody commented, and nobody asked what they meant. Then he adds the telling part: if he had misspelled one word in the body, he'd have heard plenty about it. People notice the copy. They barely register the headings, which do their work below awareness.\n\n**Secondary purpose: curiosity, maybe.** He wonders whether \"Scrambled Eggs\" made someone curious enough to start reading. He never tested it. His experience says curiosity plays a minor role. The main job stays the same: make the copy look easy.\n\n**Placement rules:**\n- Put headings in the middle of columns, not at the very top or very bottom.\n- Don't place two headings side by side in adjoining columns.\n\n**Where it ranks.** At the end of the chapter he singles out paragraph headings as the element with the least purpose beyond making copy look less imposing. Use them. Just don't spend your best thinking on them.\n\n**Translating it to screens.** On a landing page, short paragraphs and subheads do the same job, and people who skim really do read the subheads, so it's worth making them say something. In Meta primary text, line breaks and short lines do it: a wall of text in the feed gets skipped. In an email, the same. The principle hasn't changed. The reader decides in a glance whether this looks easy, and you want the answer to be yes.\n\n> **Remember it:** “Scrambled Eggs.” Nobody noticed the silly headings, but everybody would have noticed a typo. Headings make the copy look easy. That's their job.",
