      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 18 · Powerful Copy Elements Explained · #5 Product Explanation, #6 New Features · pp. 115–116 (PDF 140–141)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "explain a complicated product in a very simple way and explain a simple product in a very complex way.", page: "115" }
      ],
      idea: "**#5 Product Explanation.** It sounds too basic to list, Sugarman admits, but a surprising number of ads never explain what the product does. Then he gives a rule of thumb that is anything but basic (the quote): explain a complicated product simply, and a simple product in a complex way.\n\n**Simple product, rich explanation: the smoke detector.** Smoke detectors were common and everyone knew what they did. For an expensive brand, he told the story of what goes on inside: the gold contacts (which every detector had) and the comparator circuit that decides whether there's smoke in the room. The detector cost about **$10 more** than the going price and was a big success. Explaining the insides of an ordinary thing makes it feel special and worth more. (He returns to this ad in trigger #14, Linking, where the gold contacts become a quality link.)\n\n**Complex product, simple explanation: early computers.** When home computers were new and intimidating, his ads kept it simple: what the computer could do for you and how easy it was to use, with only a nod to the technology inside. That eased nervous buyers into a purchase. **Later**, once people understood computers and they'd become a commodity, more detail worked better. So the right level of explanation changes as the market gets more sophisticated.\n\n**The completeness check.** Whatever the level, ask: did I explain the product well enough? Have a few people read the copy, note the questions they ask, and make sure the copy answers each one.\n\n**#6 New Features.** Highlight whatever makes the product or service **new, unique or novel**. He warns that this sounds like product explanation but isn't. Explanation covers what the product does. New features are the specific things that set it apart from **everything else on the market**. One answers \"what is it?\" The other answers \"why this one?\"\n\n**How the two work together.** Explain the whole product so the reader understands it, then shine a light on the distinguishing features so the reader has a reason to pick you. Both draw on Chapter 2: you can't explain or differentiate what you haven't studied.\n\n**For a service business,** \"product explanation\" means describing the experience step by step: what happens, in what order, who does it, and what you leave with. Most local businesses treat their service as obvious, which makes it a \"simple product\" in Sugarman's terms, a candidate for richer explanation.\n\n> **Remember it:** “The smoke detector's gold contacts.” Simple product, complex story. Complex product, simple story. Then add: what's new that nobody else has?",
