      pages: "124",
      pdfPages: "149",
      title: "Copy Element #15, Physical Facts: every missing fact is an excuse not to buy",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 18 · Powerful Copy Elements Explained · #15 Physical Facts · p. 124 (PDF 149)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Give readers any excuse not to buy and they won’t buy.", page: "124" },
        { text: "List the physical dimensions even in cases where you think they are not that important.", page: "124" }
      ],
      idea: "Mention all the physical facts about the product: weight, dimensions, size, limits, speed and the like. You may decide a certain measurement doesn't really matter. Sugarman says you're wrong, and gives the reason in the first quote: give readers any excuse not to buy and they won't.\n\n**How he learned it: answering his own phones.** He used to take orders on JS&A's toll-free lines himself, on purpose. Here were customers motivated enough to pull out their money for a product they trusted him to sell, and he could listen to their unfiltered reactions in the moment of buying. That's where he discovered that missing facts kill orders. If you leave out a weight or a dimension you thought was irrelevant, people call to ask. And for every person who calls, how many didn't bother? Those people didn't order either.\n\n**Two examples:**\n- **The bathroom scale.** The ad pictured a scale on the floor but didn't give the weight of the scale itself. Who would care? Plenty of prospects did, and they kept asking. Eventually the weight went into the ad.\n- **The hand-held gadget.** He showed it in his hand and gave exact dimensions but not its weight, since weight didn't seem relevant. Again, lots of calls from people who wanted to know the weight before deciding.\n\n**The point** (second quote): list the physical facts even when you think they don't matter.\n\n**Why it works psychologically.** A buyer close to yes is looking for certainty. An unanswered question creates a small gap of uncertainty, and \"I'll find out later\" usually becomes never. A complete set of facts also signals honesty and competence: nothing is being hidden. It connects to axiom 13 (anticipate the prospect's questions and answer them) and looks ahead to trigger #29, Specificity.\n\n**The hidden lesson: listen to the questions.** Sugarman's real research method was hearing what buyers asked. Every repeated question on the phone was a fact missing from the ad. For ZDub, the equivalent is reading every DM, comment, form message and call note a client gets. The questions people ask before buying are your list of missing physical facts.\n\n**Physical facts for a service** are things like: how long it takes, exact start and end times, the address and parking, what to bring and wear, what's provided, group size, age or license requirements, what happens in heat or rain, how much noise or mess, and what you walk away with.\n\n> **Remember it:** the scale's own weight. “Who would care?” Everybody who called. Answer the boring questions before they're asked.",
      keyPoints: [
        "List all physical facts: weight, dimensions, size, limits, speed",
        "Any missing fact gives the reader an excuse not to buy",
        "Sugarman took orders himself and learned that every question asked meant others didn't call and didn't order",
        "Examples: the scale's own weight, the gadget's weight, both “irrelevant” until customers kept asking",
        "List the facts even when you think they don't matter",
        "For services: duration, times, location, what to bring, what's provided, requirements, weather policy"
      ],
      why: "PMT and local trades lose bookings to unanswered logistics: what to wear, how long it takes, where to park, whether a bike is included. The questions in a client's DMs are a free list of the facts the ads and landing page are missing.",
      example: {
        title: "PMT physical-facts block",
        before: "Beginner course available most weekends. Contact us for more information.",
        after: "**The facts.** Two days, [start and end times, confirm]. Location: [range address, parking notes]. Bikes and helmets provided. You bring: [confirm PMT's list, e.g., over-the-ankle boots, long pants, full-finger gloves, a long-sleeve jacket, eye protection]. Class size: [confirm]. Minimum age: [confirm]. Summer classes: [heat policy, water and shade]. You leave with: [the completion certificate and what it does for your license, confirm wording].",
        body: "The before forces the reader to ask, and most won't. The after answers the questions PMT gets most, in one scannable block. **Ad copy:** pick the two facts that remove the biggest hesitation (“bikes and helmets provided”) and put them in the primary text. **Landing page:** the full block, right above the Register button. **Sales call:** keep the block open while you talk so you never say “I'll have to check.” Every bracket has to be filled with PMT's real details."
      },
