      pages: "134–135",
      pdfPages: "159–160",
      title: "Trigger #2, Honesty: the single most important of the 64 points",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 19 · The Psychological Triggers · #2 Honesty · pp. 134–135 (PDF 159–160)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "If I had to pick the single most important point of the 64 points, I would pick honesty.", page: "134" },
        { text: "Consumers really appreciate the truth. And since they are smarter than you or I, you can’t fake the truth.", page: "135" }
      ],
      idea: "**What it is.** Telling the truth in your advertising, including the unflattering parts, and using that truth as a selling tool.\n\n**How much weight he gives it.** Of all 64 points in the book, this is the one he'd pick as most important (first quote). He's realistic: a dishonest ad can sometimes work, with an unbelievable price or a product that doesn't live up to its claims, maybe once or twice, but not over the long haul. And he says this section isn't really about getting away with dishonesty. It's about honesty as a **psychological selling tool**.\n\n**The premise: consumers are smart.** Smarter than you think, he says, and collectively smarter than any one of us. After decades of selling, he's convinced they can tell when someone is being truthful. The more truthful the ad, the more readily the message is accepted.\n\n**Lies leak.** Try to lie in your copy and you only fool yourself. The copy says what you meant it to say, but it also says what you tried to cover up. Even a reader skimming quickly can feel the difference. Readers pick up on hedges, gaps and overclaims even when they can't say why something feels off (second quote).\n\n**How Sugarman used it.** In his JS&A ads he regularly included the **negative features** of his products and pointed out flaws up front, then explained why they didn't amount to much and why the reader should still buy. Customers were so impressed, and trusted the message so much, that they bought eagerly. He noticed a pattern: the more truthful and frank the ad, the better the response. He calls it one of the best advertising lessons he ever learned, and made every communication truthful, whether national TV or print. (The ugly thermostat in element #8 is the classic case.)\n\n**Why it works psychologically.** Every reader of an ad is on guard, because they know the seller has a motive. Admitting a real drawback is the one thing a pure salesperson wouldn't do, so it breaks the guard down. Once the reader believes you on the bad news, they believe you on the good news. Persuasion researchers call this the effect of a two-sided message: for skeptical audiences, acknowledging the downside tends to be more persuasive than pretending there isn't one.\n\n**Honesty in the other triggers.** #3 Integrity and #4 Credibility build directly on it. Element #18 warns that fake testimonials get caught. Every trigger in this chapter works better when it's true, and backfires when it isn't.\n\n> **Remember it:** the #1 point of all 64. Flaw first, then why it doesn't matter. The consumer is smarter than you, so you can't fake it.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Sugarman picks honesty as the single most important of the 64 points",
        "Dishonesty might work once or twice, never for the long haul",
        "Consumers are smart, smarter collectively than any of us, and can sense untruth",
        "A lie in copy leaks: it says what you tried to cover up, even to a skimmer",
        "He listed his products' flaws up front, then explained why they didn't matter; the more frank, the better the response",
        "Admitting the bad makes the good believable"
      ],
      why: "Local markets run on trust and word of mouth, and every Sacramento homeowner has a story about a contractor who oversold. An ad, page or call that's honest about the hard parts (PMT's “you'll stall it,” a roofer's “you don't need a new roof yet”) stands out precisely because nobody else says it.",
      example: {
        title: "PMT: the honest version",
        before: "Learning to ride is fun and easy with us! Anyone can do it, guaranteed!",
        after: "Honest truth: you'll stall it. Probably more than once. Everyone does, and nobody on the range cares. It's also a real workout. You'll be on the bike for hours [confirm], and in July it's hot out there. But by Sunday afternoon you'll be shifting, turning and stopping on your own.",
        body: "The before makes claims a nervous reader doesn't believe (“easy,” “guaranteed”), so the guard goes up. The after admits three true drawbacks, then makes one promise, and that promise is believed because of the admissions before it. **Landing page:** a “Who this course isn't for” section (for example, experienced riders who want track training) and plain answers about heat and physical effort. **Sales call:** tell the truth even when it costs a sale. “If you've ridden for years, the beginner course will bore you; here's what fits better.” That caller tells their friends. **Trades:** “Your roof has about five good years left. You don't need a replacement today.” Confirm every fact with PMT."
      },
