      title: "Trigger #13, Timing: nobody knows if the market is ready, so test, and know the cause when timing turns",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 19 · The Psychological Triggers · #13 Timing · pp. 152–153 (PDF 177–178)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "The answer really is quite simple: Nobody knows. That’s why I always test every product that I sell first.", page: "152" },
        { text: "Timing. It can kill a product or make it.", page: "153" }
      ],
      idea: "**What it is.** Getting the moment right: launching and advertising when the market is ready, and recognizing when outside events have changed what people will respond to.\n\n**Too early, too late.** Many of his students failed because their timing was off. Timing ties closely to fads (trigger #12): get in at the beginning, not the middle or the end. But it also applies to any new product. Is the country ready for it? How do you know?\n\n**His answer** (first quote): nobody knows. That's why he tested every product before rolling it out. The consumer tells you whether you're early, late or right on time.\n\n**Timing that helps:** when crime rose, burglar alarms made sense. When the O.J. Simpson case unfolded, plenty of people, the media most of all, capitalized on it.\n\n**Timing that hurts, through no fault of yours.** In 1980, President Carter went on TV and scolded Americans for running up too much debt, urging them to stop using their credit cards. Millions did. Direct response rates collapsed overnight. Tests that had looked promising turned into losses. The timing was bad, and not because of anything JS&A did. Knowing the cause, he says, helped them keep their sanity: they didn't misread a market shock as a failed ad.\n\n**Know when timing turns.** JS&A launched the **Bone Fone**, a portable radio worn around the neck. The timing was perfect until the Sony Walkman arrived and killed it.\n\n**The lucky accident.** He once ran a media test on an electronic blood pressure monitor, and the report showed it doing remarkably well. Confident, he booked a national campaign in all his usual magazines, nearly **$300,000** at the time. Before the ads even ran, he discovered the report was wrong: the product had actually tested poorly. He braced for a big loss. But just as his ads appeared, the American Heart Association launched a major campaign urging Americans to check their blood pressure regularly. Sales jumped, the expected loss became a profit, and JS&A even won a Life Extension Award for helping alert the public. Same ads, same product. The timing changed everything (second quote).\n\n**Why it works psychologically.** The same message lands differently depending on what's already on people's minds. When the news, the season or a cultural moment makes your product's problem feel urgent, your ad is answering a question people are already asking.\n\n**Connections.** #12 Current Fads (timing a wave), #20 Sense of Urgency (creating timing inside the ad) and #14 Linking (connecting to what's in the news).\n\n> **Remember it:** Bone Fone vs. Walkman, and the $300,000 blood pressure accident. Nobody knows, so test, and always know why the numbers moved.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Being too early or too late sinks good ideas; get in at the start of a fad",
        "Nobody knows if the market is ready, so test every product first",
        "Outside events can make timing (rising crime and burglar alarms) or break it (Carter's 1980 credit card speech)",
        "Knowing the cause of a drop keeps you from misreading it",
        "The Bone Fone was killed by the Walkman",
        "The blood pressure monitor: a mistaken report led to a $300,000 campaign that profited thanks to the American Heart Association's timing"
      ],
      why: "PMT's demand is seasonal and news-sensitive, and the trades are driven by weather (heat waves, the rainy season). Planning around timing, testing small before spending big and knowing why numbers move are what keep a client from firing an agency over a market shift.",
